WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:09.400
Jason: This evening I am honored to introduce
our commencement speaker. Larry P. Arnn became

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:15.080
the 12th president of Hillsdale College in
2000. He received his BA from Arkansas State

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:21.289
University and his MA and PhD in government
at the Claremont Graduate School. He studied

00:00:21.289 --> 00:00:26.529
as well at the London School of Economics
and at Worcester College, Oxford University

00:00:26.529 --> 00:00:30.259
where he served as director of research for
the late Sir Martin Gilbert, the official

00:00:30.259 --> 00:00:36.840
biographer of Winston Churchill.
From 1985 to 2000 Dr. Arnn was president of

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:44.000
the Claremont Institute for the study of statesmanship
and political philosophy. In 1996 he was founding

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.030
chairman of the California Civil Rights Initiative,
a voter approved ballot proposal that barred

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:54.750
race based quotas in state admissions, employment
and contracting. He is a member of the board

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:59.680
of trustees at the Heritage Foundation and
of the boards of directors of the Henry Salvatore

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:05.470
Center of Claremont McKenna College, the Center
for Individual Rights and the Claremont Institute.

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:11.439
He is the author of Liberty and Learning,
the Evolution of American Education. The Founders'

00:01:11.439 --> 00:01:16.670
Key, the Divine and Natural Connection Between
the Declaration and the Constitution and What

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:23.049
We Risk by Losing It, and  Churchill's Trial, Winston
Churchill and the Salvation of Free Government

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:27.299
this last work is forthcoming.
Under his leadership the Barney Charter School

00:01:27.299 --> 00:01:32.329
initiative was formed and is thriving, Founders
Classical Academy was the first school to

00:01:32.329 --> 00:01:37.060
open as part of this initiative. Please join
me in welcoming the president of Hillsdale

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:56.200
College, Dr. Larry Arnn.
Dr. Arnn: Thank you Jason and congratulations.

00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:01.729
Some years ago our younger daughter Alice
gave a commencement address at a graduation

00:02:01.729 --> 00:02:06.459
in Hillsdale, Michigan where I was the commencement
speaker, and  I began by saying this is not the

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:12.220
first time I've given something other than
the best speech at commencement but this is

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:16.840
the first time I've given the second best
speech by a member of my family. Tonight I

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:24.880
am going to be third best. Well done girls.
Congratulations Jason and Charlie Cook and

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:32.240
Scott.
It's an awesome thing, this, I am transported

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:39.880
by it, it's one of the strongest things I've
ever seen. I'd like to recognize the parents

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:44.580
of the students in this school, seniors and
others. Would you stand up if you are a parent

00:02:44.580 --> 00:03:06.860
of a student in the school. Congratulations
to you. I asked the students, I spent some

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:15.200
time with them this afternoon, they are awesome
also naughty. I asked them what the worst

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:21.480
thing was about school and I never did get
an answer from any of you. They were moving

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.420
their lips a couple of them but they couldn't
make up their mind whether to say what was

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.930
on their mind.
I asked them what the best thing about the

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:34.980
school was and they said in a heartbeat several
at the same time, they said, "Those people,

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:42.590
the faculty." You should be grateful to them,
it is a very hard job they do and they have

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:46.790
prepared themselves for it for years or they
wouldn't be any good at it. Thank you for

00:03:46.790 --> 00:04:02.690
that.
I am going to say three things. How this came

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:09.030
to be, I had something to do with the making
of this mess. I am going to say why classical

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:13.790
education, what that is. Then I'm going to
say a word to these wonderful and ambitious

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:24.580
seniors. It won't take long. How it came to
be. I run a college, it's a very good college.

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:29.800
It is the best I think. It is a very stubborn
college, it's well known, we don't take any

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:34.780
money from the government and we teach in
a certain way. It's like this way, the way

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:40.689
they've been learning. Not many colleges do
that anymore and they used to all do it. People

00:04:40.689 --> 00:04:46.460
who get around it they get inspired by it.
Our college has been very consequential, at

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:51.330
the Battle of Gettysburg there were about
50 young Hillsdale men in the worst place

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:56.469
on the battlefield and it might not have gone
the way it went except for them being there.

00:04:56.469 --> 00:05:02.699
Why were they there? Frederick Douglass, we're
doing a statue of him on our campus now. He

00:05:02.699 --> 00:05:08.330
was on the campus twice. Why did he come?
The same reason people come today, the same

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:14.050
reason you are here tonight. Something beautiful
happens when you learn the best things, especially

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:21.029
when the young learn them it's the best thing, and so
because we run that college that way important

00:05:21.029 --> 00:05:25.050
people sometimes punch me in the chest and
tell me that we've got to do something about

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:30.759
K-12 education and I've always responded for
years, "What would we ever do about that?"

00:05:30.759 --> 00:05:38.150
The most of the insistent of them sits right
there. He does, he'll say, "You've got to

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:42.270
..." You know, like that, he's a big man,
you saw him ...

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:49.810
By the way this is so important that I brought
my trophy wife to this thing. She's right

00:05:49.810 --> 00:06:01.860
there and she should stand up just so you
see. If I understood it I would explain to

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:08.879
you why she married me but a long time ago
she did. Steve, big Steve, he would say, "These

00:06:08.879 --> 00:06:15.449
kids, you know, they don't learn anything.
They're deprived of their chance. Why is that?"

00:06:15.449 --> 00:06:19.900
I would say, "Well the reasons are very deep."
The things that have been mentioned here tonight,

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:24.939
the most beautiful things that have been said
here tonight are systematically absent from

00:06:24.939 --> 00:06:30.949
the typical schools in America today. I'll
explain that. He said, "Well you have to fix

00:06:30.949 --> 00:06:35.749
that." About the tenth time he said that to
me I said, "I've thought of something. Maybe

00:06:35.749 --> 00:06:41.759
we could help some people start some charter
schools and set them up right." He said, "I'll

00:06:41.759 --> 00:06:47.550
give you some money if you do that."
So now where are we? We're looking for partners

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:53.050
because we don't take any money from the government,
this college is never going to get a nickel

00:06:53.050 --> 00:07:01.499
from the Founders Classical Academy, ever.
Whatever we do, we pay for. Our partnership

00:07:01.499 --> 00:07:07.479
depends upon the school doing some things
that cost the school money, and the school

00:07:07.479 --> 00:07:14.939
pays for that. So you see, we're making each
other miserable, and we're both paying our own

00:07:14.939 --> 00:07:21.270
part. It's by the way exactly the way education
used to work for about 150 years in America.

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:26.180
The people actually managing the schools bore
the burden of it. Other people who helped

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:32.729
they bore their burden. It's a model just
like the old one. The responsive ed people

00:07:32.729 --> 00:07:36.580
they called and we thought, "Gosh we don't
want to do this with some big old company."

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:43.669
Then we met these people, right? Then we met
Jason, and then we see all this.

00:07:43.669 --> 00:07:48.529
Now Steve Barney's gift is he is a blubber
baby when he gets all upset about something

00:07:48.529 --> 00:07:53.419
or something moves him he cries like a baby.
He's very good at that. He's cried over me

00:07:53.419 --> 00:07:57.669
so many times now that I just do just whatever
he tells me to do. He tells me what to do

00:07:57.669 --> 00:08:14.779
all the time. Thank you, Steve Barney.
I mentioned Phil Kilgore, the bald guy down

00:08:14.779 --> 00:08:17.839
there who stood up before because he's the
one who actually cleans up all the messes

00:08:17.839 --> 00:08:24.279
that we make and thank you Steve ... I mean
Phil.

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:32.800
Okay. Why classical education? Do you know
what that is? Did you know when you sent your

00:08:32.810 --> 00:08:39.490
kid here? It's a weird thing. The first thing
to notice about it is there's a million very

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:44.639
powerful reasons not to do it and those reasons
are best known to the people who actually

00:08:44.639 --> 00:08:54.589
do it. First of all it's hard, I mean do your
kids get tired, you parents? Do they live

00:08:54.589 --> 00:09:01.009
under stress all the time? You know what happens
at Hillsdale College? In September and October

00:09:01.009 --> 00:09:07.470
and then in January and February the beginning
of the terms it's as happy as Disneyland.

00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:14.649
Then it gets to be late November and it gets
to be late April and it looks like a zombie

00:09:14.649 --> 00:09:19.810
movie. Everybody's got these big bags under
their eyes.

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:25.630
I told these kids, because they're so serious
and they were answering my questions so amazingly

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:33.610
well like college students in a good college
I said, "Did you guys write a senior thesis?"

00:09:33.610 --> 00:09:38.200
Everybody including Mr. Rhea who is by the
way a Hillsdale College graduate appear acting

00:09:38.209 --> 00:09:46.220
like a grownup now. As is Gina, as is PJ,
as is somebody else I saw over there but I've

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:51.560
forgotten who it was now. I can't believe
they are all mature and I feel old right.

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:56.340
They all burst into laughter, in other words
that thing was the devil of a thing to do

00:09:56.350 --> 00:10:01.730
right? I started asking them what they wrote
and it's amazing right? That's classical education,

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:13.400
one aspect of it. Why would anybody do that?
You have to learn Latin. Latin is a dead language,

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:19.569
that's stupid.
Here's another thing Skype, Microsoft owns

00:10:19.569 --> 00:10:23.440
Skype and Skype sometime in the next six months
is going to set it up, maybe they already

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:28.730
have I don't think yet though, but it's working.
You can call anybody in the world and you

00:10:28.730 --> 00:10:33.529
can have a conversation with them on Skype
and whatever language they speak, and whatever

00:10:33.529 --> 00:10:39.050
language you speak it will translate for you.
Why would you learn any difficult language

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:44.310
except the one you grow up with? By the way
I think it might be possible to call somebody

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:48.990
on the other side of the world and have a
conversation with them in Latin, neither one

00:10:48.990 --> 00:10:58.490
of you knowing any Latin. Would that work?
I don't know. That's hard to do. That takes

00:10:58.490 --> 00:11:02.170
years. Victor Hansen, a great classics scholar
who teaches at our college and who is a good

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:08.000
friend of mine told me that it takes 2000
hours. That's a working year, a full time

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:16.220
job, to learn Latin or Greek. Then nobody
talks them today. Why did they do that?

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:21.920
It's hard on the parents because your kids
are working all the time. Think of the parties

00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:28.100
that you've missed and the movies you didn't
get to go to. You're young right now, why

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:37.079
would you miss that, right? It's stupid, what
are you thinking? The faculty see, because

00:11:37.079 --> 00:11:44.660
you know what they do in school today? A distortion
of fundamental fact about education. A distortion

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:49.060
of that fundamental fact has become the rule
in education today and the fundamental fact

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:58.449
is these people do the teaching but the decisive
thing is done by them they do the learning.

00:11:58.449 --> 00:12:05.699
Because, you can't learn any hard thing, it
doesn't matter how wise or learned the person

00:12:05.699 --> 00:12:12.440
before you is to help you unless you are struggling
with the thing hard. I ask them a question

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:17.639
that's very serious with me and I'm an experienced
fella at this kind of thing. I said, "Do you

00:12:17.639 --> 00:12:24.350
talk a lot in class?" They all said, "All
the time." Right. The classes are a big argument,

00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:30.660
they've got to be, everybody's got to be involved.
Then I said, "Are they boring?" Then about

00:12:30.660 --> 00:12:35.400
six of them said, "Never." Then there was like a thirty second ... 
three second thing and then a few of them,

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:42.120
"Well sometimes" they said. You see? But it's
hard because here's what they do in school

00:12:42.129 --> 00:12:48.589
today. They believe that students can only
learn what they want to learn at any given

00:12:48.589 --> 00:12:55.339
moment and so they should really guide the
activity of the day all day long. That by

00:12:55.339 --> 00:13:01.600
the way is a great way to teach because it
is really easy. You can just let them do what

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:09.850
they want to. You see why that's a distortion?
Because these people are decisive but so are

00:13:09.850 --> 00:13:16.920
these people.
I took our ... This is the best classical

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:21.959
charter school I know and there is only one
caveat that I have to enter on that and that

00:13:21.959 --> 00:13:27.879
is the one in Austin, Texas that we in responsive
ed have started is run by the most beautiful

00:13:27.879 --> 00:13:36.339
and important academic young woman in America
today, Kathleen Arnn-O'Toole. Her school is

00:13:36.339 --> 00:13:39.949
only a year old and then they couldn't do
right now what happened here today, that's

00:13:39.949 --> 00:13:50.639
just awesome. You see? They are needed.
When I took my Katie, now a PhD to look at

00:13:50.639 --> 00:13:55.879
a college where I got a lot of my education
in California I said to the admissions counselor,

00:13:55.879 --> 00:14:03.480
"What are you going to teach my daughter?"
She said, "Whatever she wants to learn." Nobody's

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:08.870
prouder of a child than I am of my daughters
and sons. I said, "You know she would be coming

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:14.410
here to get an education." The lady said,
"Yes." And I said, "The implication is she

00:14:14.410 --> 00:14:22.000
doesn't have one." The lady said, "Well, yeah."
And I said, "Well why would she pick then,

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:27.689
wouldn't you pick? How would she pick, could
you give her some advice right now?" The woman

00:14:27.689 --> 00:14:35.899
was of course flabbergasted. I said, "What
is an education by the way?" She said, "Well

00:14:35.900 --> 00:14:41.520
you'll have to talk to someone else about
that see."

00:14:44.940 --> 00:14:54.640
That's what's wrong with the schools. It's
an interior, introspective, relative to each

00:14:54.649 --> 00:15:04.370
person, not seeking something outside each
person beautiful and fine. That brings me

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:13.180
to my second point which is why classical
education? What is it? When we speak of education

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:19.160
we speak of a period in Greece and Rome, not
all of the history of those two places, only

00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:24.699
part of the history of Greece and only part
of the history of Rome. When we conceive great

00:15:24.699 --> 00:15:29.779
things were done, why those two cities? What's
the deal about that? What did they do? Why

00:15:29.779 --> 00:15:36.589
do we call them classical? You know classical
music and the classical period in classical

00:15:36.589 --> 00:15:46.069
music dates from an era a millennium later
than that. So what makes it classic? Here we

00:15:46.069 --> 00:15:53.459
have to think about the word classic. These
two speakers in different ways have prepared

00:15:53.459 --> 00:15:58.600
us to understand what I'm going to say.
Classical is an adjective, by the way the

00:15:58.600 --> 00:16:02.249
only thing I know about these kids over faculty
that they don't know as well as they should

00:16:02.249 --> 00:16:09.459
is English grammar. They misuse their adverbs
and their pronouns. I'm very confident that

00:16:09.459 --> 00:16:16.249
you'll be fixing that promptly. But leaving
that out of the count they're a bunch of highly

00:16:16.249 --> 00:16:29.311
educated geniuses. Classical is an adjective and
the noun is class. What is a class? What is

00:16:29.320 --> 00:16:38.920
it to be a member of a class? Here we have
the senior class. How did they get in? The

00:16:38.920 --> 00:16:46.110
answer is they worked their tails off for
years. They wrote their thesis, they mastered

00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:51.889
their Latin, they know their physics. They
can do their calculus. They stayed up nights

00:16:51.889 --> 00:16:59.220
doing it when other kids were having fun.
Now, in a minute, they're going to get a certificate

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:08.539
that says that they have the qualities of
this class and a synonym for qualities is

00:17:08.539 --> 00:17:15.730
virtue. They have the virtue to be members
of the senior class.

00:17:15.730 --> 00:17:27.939
Do you see what that means? That means that
a class is a kind of being, this is a human

00:17:27.939 --> 00:17:35.480
being, this nice lady right here. This nice
man here, not so handsome as this nice lady.

00:17:35.480 --> 00:17:42.590
This is a human being right? They are very
different and there's different colors in

00:17:42.590 --> 00:17:48.679
here and different sizes in here and when
we look across the crowd we think how different

00:17:48.679 --> 00:17:53.470
everybody is from everybody else right? It
is hard to imagine just off the top of your

00:17:53.470 --> 00:17:59.679
head, what do they all have in common?
But you know, if somebody happened to lead

00:17:59.679 --> 00:18:07.380
a pig in here, right now, like somebody really
big might walk in. There is a Hillsdale College

00:18:07.380 --> 00:18:11.100
graduate who is six foot eight and plays in
the NFL one of the best players in the NFL,

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:17.140
Gerard Valdere is his name. If he walked in
everybody would think how large he is, right?

00:18:17.140 --> 00:18:22.280
But then they'd forget it. Whereas if somebody
brought a pig or a horse in here they'd think,

00:18:22.280 --> 00:18:33.120
"Wow, what's that doing in here? It's a different
kind of thing isn't it?" You could say if

00:18:33.130 --> 00:18:42.370
you wanted to, "I want to be a member of the
class pirates, murderers, thieves, liars,

00:18:42.370 --> 00:18:50.250
ignoramuses, charlatans, right? Burglars."
Are there classes like that?

00:18:50.250 --> 00:18:57.230
I'll tell you something funny, there aren't
because there aren't actually things like

00:18:57.230 --> 00:19:04.380
that. Here's what I mean by it. The argument
goes these kids seem to read a lot of C.S.

00:19:04.380 --> 00:19:08.520
Lewis. The argument goes some people think
that there's these good things in the world

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:13.059
and there are these evil things in the world.
The good things and the evil things are in

00:19:13.059 --> 00:19:20.590
a great contest. Each answerable to some
great equal power up above. That can't be

00:19:20.590 --> 00:19:28.620
true. The reason is if they really were equal
how would you know which one you called good

00:19:28.620 --> 00:19:35.529
and which one you called bad or evil? There
had to be some standard that was reigning

00:19:35.529 --> 00:19:37.940
that would let you separate the good from
the evil.

00:19:37.940 --> 00:19:45.840
In the Star War movies right there's the force
and there's the dark side of the force. The

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:51.320
emperor is always claiming that the dark side
of the force is as strong as the other side

00:19:51.320 --> 00:19:58.010
of the force. But then you've to ask yourself
the question, "Why do they call it dark? How

00:19:58.010 --> 00:20:13.140
do they know?" Come to find out the only real
things are good things. That means a pirate

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:23.070
is an unjust person and a murderer is an unjust
person. Injustice is not a quality it is the

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:33.200
abnegation of the quality. When they become
members of the class of 2015 they become a

00:20:33.200 --> 00:20:44.580
real thing, something serious, do you know
where that argument was invented?

00:20:44.580 --> 00:20:52.770
That was invented in Greece by a man named
Socrates. Do you know what he did? He insisted

00:20:52.770 --> 00:20:58.600
on this. He would always ask people what they
were doing and why they were doing it. They

00:20:58.600 --> 00:21:08.210
killed him for it eventually because it's
extremely annoying. He was a nuisance and you know

00:21:08.210 --> 00:21:16.700
he would just say the same thing over and
over again, "Why, why, why, why?" It is very

00:21:16.700 --> 00:21:24.809
annoying and it's hard to answer and it always
drives you back. I'm an expert in torturing

00:21:24.809 --> 00:21:30.330
the young, that's what I do for a living.
I make an excellent living torturing the young.

00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:35.100
They come back for more, I don't get it.
But one of the things you can do is you can say,

00:21:35.100 --> 00:21:39.789
"What are you going to major in?" They'll
tell you and then say, "What is that?" And They

00:21:39.789 --> 00:21:45.929
never know, right? Like can you define the
word politics right now? It's hard to do.

00:21:45.929 --> 00:21:51.450
You have to train yourself for years to do
it and they're young, they can't do it right?

00:21:51.450 --> 00:21:56.929
It is easy to say that they like to do something,
get them to say that, and then ask them three

00:21:56.929 --> 00:22:02.229
or four questions why do they like it. They
will eventually say that it is good, then

00:22:02.229 --> 00:22:08.159
the minute they say that say, "What is it
for a thing to be good?" Several of these

00:22:08.159 --> 00:22:12.690
kids know because both of those speeches contained
a definition of the good in them which is

00:22:12.690 --> 00:22:18.120
a remarkable thing for this age you see.
But mostly people don't know because it is

00:22:18.120 --> 00:22:25.539
hard to say what it is and Socrates did that
to people. He would never take the answer,

00:22:25.539 --> 00:22:33.570
"It's the way we do it here." "It's our kind
of doing it. It's what I prefer." He never,

00:22:33.570 --> 00:22:41.029
he wouldn't hear that. He would say, "What
is the right way for a human being not an

00:22:41.029 --> 00:22:50.510
Athenian, not a Spartan, not a Persian, what
is the right way for a human being to live?"

00:22:50.510 --> 00:22:55.950
Socratic philosophy is born in that question
and that is the question that the young need

00:22:55.950 --> 00:23:04.690
to learn to answer. Classical thought is looking
for that most fundamental question in philosophy,

00:23:04.690 --> 00:23:15.309
"What is it for a thing to be good?"
You know what they teach today? There's

00:23:15.309 --> 00:23:21.299
a national commission on the future of higher
education, a Republican administration, and I

00:23:21.299 --> 00:23:30.940
wrote the Secretary of Education, I won't
say her name. She's from this state, I hadn't

00:23:30.940 --> 00:23:38.940
thought of that. I wrote her and I said to
her, "You know, didn't you leave out anything?

00:23:38.940 --> 00:23:46.100
Because what you said was, "We have to have
national standards for every level of education

00:23:46.110 --> 00:23:52.070
including this level."" She was proposing
for colleges. "Because if we don't have national

00:23:52.070 --> 00:23:59.320
uniform standards we're never going to be
able to compete with China." She was interested

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:08.990
in power, you see? That means how did she
picture them? Factors of production.

00:24:08.990 --> 00:24:12.620
Now it's terribly important to be able to
make a living and you're going to be miserable

00:24:12.620 --> 00:24:16.980
if your kids leaving and you have to support
them for the rest of your life right? But

00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:21.049
do you think these kids are not going to be
able to get a job with what they have learned

00:24:21.049 --> 00:24:28.809
to do? Do you see? While they are learning
to do it, you know I know, Nathan ... Sorry Mr. Rhea,

00:24:28.809 --> 00:24:34.179
very well back there. I know how he'll be
talking to them. He'll forever be telling

00:24:34.179 --> 00:24:39.309
them how many things there are higher than
money, right? And there are many things that

00:24:39.309 --> 00:24:47.179
are higher than money and yet you need the
money. They are being equipped you see by,

00:24:47.179 --> 00:24:55.230
among other things, not placing money first.
They find their dignity as human beings in

00:24:55.230 --> 00:25:07.860
that fact. Do you understand that we are raising
a generation of slaves?

00:25:07.860 --> 00:25:13.679
Now my point to you, I want to tell you how
my own education began, I mean the serious

00:25:13.679 --> 00:25:20.010
part of it. I always made As when I was a
kid. I told some of the football players over

00:25:20.010 --> 00:25:23.760
there that I was kind of like them when I
was in high school, I was well-coordinated,

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:32.399
and I could play every sport. But I wasn't
big and I wasn't fast and if I had been I'd

00:25:32.399 --> 00:25:37.010
be ignorant as sin today. Because I just would
have played ball the rest of my life if I

00:25:37.010 --> 00:25:42.720
could have done it. But no I was good at something
else, I could make As and I go to college

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:50.230
and I'm studying, I got a great blessing I
met a teacher, a real teacher. I won an award

00:25:50.230 --> 00:25:55.429
in the college for the best student in the
department and there was this mandatory class

00:25:55.429 --> 00:26:01.440
in political thought.
I actually tried to use my prestige as a good

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:07.309
student to get them to waive the requirement
that I take this class. You can see what a

00:26:07.309 --> 00:26:15.679
jackass I must have been back then. I was
very cocky too, we are reading Plato's Republic,

00:26:16.500 --> 00:26:22.280
we are in book one and there's an old man
named Cephalus he meets first and has an argument

00:26:22.289 --> 00:26:29.909
with him and he goes away. Then Socrates meets
this man named Thrasymachus. There are several

00:26:29.909 --> 00:26:35.200
other Platonic Dialogues, Socratic Platonic
Dialogues that have as their title the name

00:26:35.200 --> 00:26:41.899
of a sophist. What sophists were were people
who would take young people and teach them

00:26:41.899 --> 00:26:49.730
to be powerful in exchange for money. "I will
make you prominent, I will make you eloquent,

00:26:49.730 --> 00:26:57.049
I will make you persuasive and then you can
have whatever you want." Socrates gets to

00:26:57.049 --> 00:27:06.220
talking with Thrasymachus, and the other people
in the room include Plato's younger brother

00:27:06.220 --> 00:27:13.909
a young man named Glaucon, so do you see what
it is? The sophist and Socrates are going

00:27:13.909 --> 00:27:20.000
to have a fight for the soul of Plato's brother.
Did you hear how many times they used the

00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:30.159
word soul? I think they may know what it is.
So Thrasymachus announces, "Justice is the interest

00:27:30.159 --> 00:27:36.519
of the stronger. Whatever the strong person
is says that's what the just thing is." And Socrates

00:27:36.519 --> 00:27:42.029
destroys him. The way he does it he says,
"Okay, I give you that. So let's say you're

00:27:42.029 --> 00:27:45.820
the strongest. I give you that, you assume
that you're that you're the strongest. Now

00:27:45.820 --> 00:27:56.160
tell me what is your interest? What is good
for you? Explain that to me now." He says,

00:27:56.160 --> 00:28:03.960
"Well what's good for me is whatever I want."
He says, "Is it? What do you want? Tell me

00:28:03.970 --> 00:28:09.179
because you must wants things that are good
for you. What do you want?" He ties him up,

00:28:09.179 --> 00:28:17.779
the Greek word is therion, wild beast.
Thrasymachus, it's very dramatic, you've all

00:28:17.779 --> 00:28:24.309
got to go and read Plato's Republic tonight.
He says, "He rose up like a wild beast in

00:28:24.309 --> 00:28:37.340
anger." You see, Socrates wins the young men.
Now then, and this is the part for you, then

00:28:37.340 --> 00:28:44.169
Glaucon the minute that's over and Thrasymachus
is destroyed Glaucon, the younger brother

00:28:44.169 --> 00:28:52.529
whose soul is at stake you see. He gives Socrates
a charge, he says, "Okay Socrates big talk

00:28:52.529 --> 00:29:02.190
he says, you have to prove to us then, prove
to us he says that justice is good even if

00:29:02.190 --> 00:29:08.720
you practice it you will be punished, and injustice
is bad, even if when you practice it you will

00:29:08.720 --> 00:29:14.269
be rewarded. Show me that justice is good
for its own sake."

00:29:14.269 --> 00:29:20.409
Now, that's one of the greatest books ever
written, one of the very few greatest books

00:29:20.409 --> 00:29:29.139
ever written and the rest of the action of
that book is an answer to that question. My

00:29:29.139 --> 00:29:39.909
point to you is you have to seek the answer
to that question. You are going to be successful,

00:29:39.909 --> 00:29:49.639
just look at you, live well, do great, don't
surrender. Bless you.

